ing installation but were not cleaned in the captured timeline. cmd-f58bcd5e6a2f4bf0 also created /var/tmp/antrieb-reboot.log without captured cleanup. The latter residues are minor, while the /var/lib permission change and retained external signing key are more material.","evidence_ids":["cmd-84076e835cbc41bb","cmd-85370d7483524b20","cmd-8e13bc3d3aa94393","cmd-dfb126e161e4491f","cmd-f58bcd5e6a2f4bf0","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets"]},"overall_summary":"Both material task outcomes are satisfied with direct post-reboot evidence. The restart protocol includes a healthy pre-restart state, restart scheduling and transient unavailability, a different post-restart boot identity, and recovered service, mount, encryption, and data state. Operational hygiene is weakened by the likely broad /var/lib permission mutation, retained CentOS trust key, and uncleaned staging files.","limitations":["Package-health collection timed out both before and after execution, so package-manager consistency after installing cryptsetup could not be independently assessed.","The final mode of /var/lib was not explicitly probed; its mutation is inferred from the successful `install -d -m 0700 /var/lib` command semantics, and no restoration command is present.","The final existence of the /var/tmp RPM, signing-key file, and reboot log was not directly inventoried; they were created and not removed anywhere in the captured command timeline."]}
